course: french
lesson: 20
title: In the classroom
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your pencil case, une trousse, holds the first French words you will use every day: un stylo (a pen), un crayon (a pencil), une gomme (a rubber) and une règle (a ruler).
explain: Watch the traps: un crayon is a pencil, not a wax crayon, une gomme is a rubber, not chewing gum, and une trousse is a pencil case, not trousers.
explain: The teacher gives orders with vous because she is talking to the whole class: écoutez (listen), regardez (look), levez la main (put your hand up), asseyez-vous (sit down).
explain: Picture Ethan in Cardiff hearing Levez la main ! and standing up: levez-vous means stand up, but levez la main only asks for your hand.
explain: Saying Je ne comprends pas (I don't understand) is smart, not silly: every language learner needs it, together with Comment dit-on... en français ? (How do you say... in French?).
fact: une gomme ("GOMM") is a rubber or eraser; chewing gum is un chewing-gum.
fact: un crayon ("kray-YON") is a pencil and un stylo ("stee-LO") is a pen; colouring pencils are des crayons de couleur and wax crayons are des crayons de cire.
fact: Je peux aller aux toilettes, s'il vous plaît ? means "May I go to the toilet, please?"; toilettes is plural in French.
fact: Classroom orders use vous: écoutez (listen), regardez (look), levez la main (put your hand up), levez-vous (stand up), asseyez-vous (sit down).
fact: Je ne comprends pas means "I don't understand"; Vous pouvez répéter, s'il vous plaît ? means "Can you repeat, please?"
fact: Comment dit-on « skateboard » en français ? means "How do you say 'skateboard' in French?"; the answer is un skateboard, or un skate.
fact: Young children in France call their primary teacher la maîtresse (a woman) or le maître (a man); older pupils say madame or monsieur.
fact: un cahier ("ka-YAY") is an exercise book; the h is silent; un livre is a book you read.
fact: Little phrases help you speak French every lesson: J'ai fini ! (I've finished!), C'est facile (it's easy), C'est difficile (it's hard).
fact: Teacher comments: Très bien ! (very good), Bravo ! (well done), Excellent ! (excellent), Attention ! (careful).
fact: des ciseaux ("see-ZOH") are scissors and are plural, as in English; la colle is glue and une règle is a ruler.
fact: une trousse ("TROOSS") is a pencil case; inside you might find a taille-crayon (a sharpener).
fact: Slang here: "mind blown" means amazed; "honey" (Texas) is a friendly way to talk to a child; "grand" (Ireland) means great; "la" (Liverpool) means mate; "chur" (New Zealand) means thanks or cool.
exercise: Oliver can't find his rubber. What should he say? | Où est ma gomme ? | Où est mon gomme ? | Où est mon rubber ? | Où est ma chewing-gum ?
exercise: What is un crayon in French? | a pencil | a wax crayon | a pen | a felt-tip
exercise: What is un stylo? | a pen | a pencil | a ruler | a style of hair
exercise: Aisha needs the toilet. What should she ask? | Je peux aller aux toilettes, s'il vous plaît ? | Je peux aller à la toilette, s'il te plaît, madame ? | Toilet, please, Miss ? | Je veux toilette maintenant !
exercise: What does Levez la main ! mean? | Put your hand up! | Stand up! | Wash your hands! | Sit down!
exercise: Which order means "sit down"? | asseyez-vous | levez-vous | levez la main | regardez
exercise: What does écoutez mean? | listen | look | write | stand up
exercise: Lily doesn't understand. What should she say? | Je ne comprends pas. | Je ne sais pas parler. | Je n'ai pas fini. | Je ne regarde pas.
exercise: How do you ask the teacher to say it again? | Vous pouvez répéter, s'il vous plaît ? | Tu peux répéter, toi ? | Vous pouvez parler plus fort, merci beaucoup ? | Répète, maintenant !
exercise: Max wants a French word for skateboard. What should he ask? | Comment dit-on « skateboard » en français ? | Comment tu t'appelles, mon skateboard, en anglais ? | Où est le skateboard en français ? | Quel âge a le skateboard ?
exercise: Who is la maîtresse to a little French child? | the woman primary teacher | the head of the whole town | the dinner lady | the school nurse
exercise: How does un cahier sound? | "uhn ka-YAY" | "uhn KAY-ak" | "uhn ka-HEER" | "uhn ka-HEE-er"
exercise: What is un cahier? | an exercise book | a book you read at bedtime | a kayak | a pencil case
exercise: Jessica holds up her work. What can she say? | J'ai fini ! | Je suis fini ! | J'ai finir ! | Je fini !
exercise: What does Très bien ! on a test mean? | very good | very bad | try again | three good answers
exercise: Harvey needs scissors. What should he ask for? | des ciseaux | les scissors | le ciseau | des Caesars
exercise: What is une trousse? | a pencil case | a pair of trousers | a school bag | a ruler
test: Oliver says, "Gum? I'm not chewing it." What is the truth about une gomme? | It is a rubber for rubbing out | It is chewing gum, so he must chew it | It is a glue stick | It is a sticky sweet
test: Sophie says, "So crayon's a pencil? Mind blown." ("Mind blown" means amazed.) What are wax crayons in French? | des crayons de cire | des crayons | des stylos de cire | des gommes de couleur
test: Aisha panics when her teacher says "En français !" What could happen if she learns only one sentence first? | She can always ask for the toilet politely | She will be told off every day | She will never need the toilet | She will forget all her English
test: Ethan stands up when he hears "Levez la main !" The teacher laughs, "Raise the hand, not the boy." Which order did Ethan think he heard? | levez-vous | asseyez-vous | écoutez | regardez
test: Lily's teacher says, "Just say you don't understand, honey, in French!" ("Honey" is a friendly word.) Why is it smart? | It gets you help instead of pretending | It makes the teacher stop the lesson for good | It means you won't get homework | It is the only French you need
test: Max says, "Grand, now I can ask for anything." ("Grand" means great.) Why is Comment dit-on...? so useful? | It unlocks any word you don't know | It tells you the time | It is how you say goodbye | It asks for the toilet
test: Grace asks, "Is la maîtresse like a headteacher?" What's the truth? | Little children call their primary teacher la maîtresse | It's the head of all the primary schools in the whole of France | It means the dinner lady | It's what you call your mum at school
test: Josh asks, "Why's my book a kayak?" His teacher says, "It's cah-YAY, mate." What is wrong with "kayak"? | The h is silent and -ier says "yay" | The k is silent and -ier says "ak" | It is said "ka-HEER", like hear | Nothing, cahier is said exactly like the boat called a kayak
test: Jessica's friend says, "Me too, la, but I'm not saying it in French." ("La" means mate.) How would she say "it's easy"? | C'est facile. | C'est difficile. | J'ai fini. | Très bien.
test: Harvey laughs, "Sounds like Caesar's." Why is it des ciseaux, not un ciseau? | Scissors are plural in French too | The teacher had two pairs | ciseaux is the word for knives | Caesar owned the scissors
test: Leo says, "Chur, not wearing my pencil case, mate." ("Chur" means cool.) Which is in une trousse? | un stylo, un crayon, une gomme | un pantalon, un short, un jean | un cahier, un livre, un sac | un chapeau, des gants, un bonnet
test: ★★★ The teacher says: "Regardez, écoutez, puis levez la main." What do you do, in order? | look, listen, then put your hand up | listen, look, then stand up | look, listen, then sit down | stand up, look, then listen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the French noun crayon? | It denotes a pencil, wax crayons being termed crayons de cire | It denotes a wax crayon exclusively | It denotes a fountain pen | It denotes a rubber
exam: Identify the correct request for permission to visit the lavatory. | Je peux aller aux toilettes, s'il vous plaît ? | Je veux aller à la toilette tout de suite, monsieur ! | Toilette, monsieur ? | Je vais toilettes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the instruction Levez la main. | It requires that the pupil raise a hand | It requires that the pupil stand | It requires that the pupil wash both hands | It requires that the pupil leave the room
exam: It is given that a pupil does not comprehend an explanation. Determine the expression appropriate to that circumstance. | Je ne comprends pas | J'ai fini | C'est facile | Très bien
